 <u> SUMMARY </u> :  Establishes the California Peer Assistance and Review  
Program for Teachers for the purpose of assisting veteran  
teachers in need of development in subject matter knowledge or  
teaching strategies. Specifically, <u> this bill: </u> 

1)Sunsets the California Mentor Teacher (CMT) Program July 1,  
  2000 and establishes in its place the California Peer  
  Assistance and Review Program (PAR), which would become fully  
  operational on the same date.


2)Specifies that a school district that accepts state funds for  
  this program agrees to negotiate the development and  
  implementation of the program with the exclusive  
  representative of the certificated employees in the school  
  district, if there is an exclusive representative of the  
  certificated employees.


3)Specifies that a school district that elects not to  
  participate in this program is not eligible for funding for  
  PAR or for:  a) the Beginning Teacher Support and Assessment  
  (BTSA) Program; b) the Instructional Time and Staff  
  Development Reform Program; and c) school development plans.   
  Also requires districts not participating in this program to  
  report annually at a regularly scheduled meeting of the school  
  district governing board on the rationale for not  








                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  2

  participating in the program.


4)Specifies that a district that receives funding for  
  participating in the PAR Program may also use the funding for  
  BTSA and the employment of district interns.


5)Requires, at a minimum, the following principles to be  
  included in locally developed peer review programs:


   a)   Teacher participants in the program who have permanent  
     status and volunteer to participate or be referred for  
     participation in the program as a result of their biennial  
     evaluation;

   b)   Written and clearly stated performance goals for  
     individual teachers that are aligned with pupil learning  
     goals;  

   c)   Review and assistance that includes multiple  
     observations of a teacher during periods of classroom  
     instruction;

    d)  Sufficient staff development activities to assist  
     teachers to improve their teaching skills and knowledge;

   e)   A monitoring component with a written record; and

   f)   The final evaluation of a teacher's participation in the  
     program must be made available for placement in the  
     teacher's personnel file.


6)Requires a mentor teacher participating in this program to:   
  a) be a credentialed classroom teacher with permanent status;  
  b) have substantial recent experience in classroom  
  instruction; and c) have demonstrated exemplary teaching  
  ability.  "Exemplary teaching ability" is indicated by  
  effective communication skills, subject matter knowledge and  
  mastery of a range of teaching strategies.


7)Requires a joint teacher administrator peer review panel to be  








                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  3

  established by participating districts to govern the PAR  
  program. The panel must select mentors for the program, review  
  peer review reports, and make recommendations to the school  
  district governing board regarding program participants. This  
  duty would include forwarding to the governing board the names  
  of individuals who are not able to demonstrate satisfactory  
  improvement after getting assistance.


8)Requires the joint peer review panel to annually evaluate the  
  impact of the PAR Program.  The panel may submit  
  recommendations for improvement of the program to the district  
  governing board and to the exclusive representative of the  
  certificated employees of the district, if there is an  
  exclusive representative of the certificated employees.


9)Requires school district governing boards to evaluate and  
  assess certificated employee performance as it reasonably  
  relates to the progress of pupils toward the state-adopted  
  academic content standards as measured by state-adopted  
  criterion referenced assessments.  Authorizes a school  
  district to require that a certificated employee who receives  
  an unsatisfactory rating in this evaluation to participate in  
  its Mentor Peer Review and Assistance (MPRA) Program.


10)Requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction (SPI),  
  subject to the availability of funding in the annual Budget  
  Act, to contract with an independent evaluator on or before  
  December 15, 2002 to prepare a comprehensive evaluation of the  
  implementation, impact, cost, and benefit of the PAR program.   
  This evaluation would be required to be delivered to the  
  Legislature, the Governor, and interested parties on or before  
  January 1, 2004.


 <u> EXISTING LAW </u> : 

1)Establishes the CMT Program and provides that the primary  
  function of a mentor teacher is to provide assistance and  
  guidance to new teachers.  Mentor teachers may provide staff  
  development for teachers and develop special curriculum. 










                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  4

2)Requires the governing board of each school district to  
  evaluate and assess certificated employee performance as it  
  reasonably relates to the progress of pupils toward the  
  standards of expected pupil achievement established by the  
  governing board.


 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :  Appropriates $100 million (Proposition 98) from  
the General Fund for the 1999-2000 fiscal year, with $16.8  
million for the MPRA Program and $83.2 million for the existing   
CMT Program. Requires SPI to apportion to participating school  
districts in the 1999-2000 fiscal year $1,000 for each mentor  
teacher funded by the state in 1999-2000.  Districts may use  
this funding for staff development activities and training  
necessary to implement the MPRA Program.

 <u> COMMENTS </u> :   This bill is one of four bills sponsored by the  
Governor for the special legislative session on education.  The  
other bills are AB 2X (Mazzoni), pending in the Assembly,  which  
would establish the Elementary School Intensive Reading Program,  
SB 1X (Alpert), pending in the Senate, which would establish the  
Public School Performance Accountability Program, and SB 2X  
(O'Connell), pending in the Senate, which would create a high  
school exit examination.
 

